                <!-- ===================================================== -->
                <!-- SUMMARY -->
                <!-- ===================================================== -->

                <h3 class="h3 fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Summary
                </h3>

                <div class="alert alert-primary border-0 shadow-sm mb-4">

                    <ul class="mb-0">

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            A <b>class</b> is a blueprint or definition for objects.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            An <b>object</b> is an instance of a class.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            A class can contain fields, properties, and methods.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            Objects are commonly created using the <b>new</b> keyword.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            Multiple objects can be created from the same class.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            Each object can maintain its own instance data.
                        </li>

                        <li class="mb-2">
                            Instance members are accessed through objects.
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            Classes and objects form the foundation of
                            Object-Oriented Programming in C#.
                        </li>

                    </ul>

                </div>


